p.src:
Gauss’s theorem is different. It is a mathematical identity valid for any sufficiently regular vector field and any region $V$ bounded by the closed surface $\partial V$: $\boxed{
  \int_V
    \boldsymbol{\nabla}\cdot\vec A\,dV
  =
  \oint_{\partial V}
    \vec A\cdot\hat n\,d\sigma.}$ The region need not be spherical. Subdivide its volume into tiny cells, evaluate the divergence in each, multiply by the cell volume, and add. Internal fluxes cancel between neighboring cells, leaving only the outward normal component $A_\perp=\vec A\cdot\hat n$ on the boundary.

