p.src:
The lecture makes an uncertain ten-minute estimate. Evaluating the model instead gives $T
  =
  \frac{2\pi}{\omega}
  =
  2\pi\sqrt{\frac{R}{g_{\mathrm s}}}
  \approx 84\ \mathrm{min}.$ The trip from one surface to the opposite surface is half a period, about $42$ minutes; the fall from the surface to the center is about $21$ minutes. The numerical correction leaves the point of the example unchanged: Gauss’s law solves a many-body problem which would be forbidding as a direct sum.

